Owner-approved (2026-08-19): "yes, update the playbook. I approve and I
approve using auto-pr without --gov". Recorded on WI-sipuk.

The playbook documented one calibration run and two measurement
artifacts. The 2026-08-18 run supplies better numbers at the SAME
thresholds and found two further artifacts, one blind spot, and three
operational hazards — all of which cost time in that run.

CALIBRATION. Both runs now tabulated: 2026-05-15 (28 flagged / 21 real
/ 75%) and 2026-08-18 (45 / 41 / 91%). Thresholds unchanged. Also
records that a fixed file drops OUT of the next pool on its own,
because correcting a module docstring resets `ds` — so the second
run's 45-file pool correctly excluded the 15 files fixed hours earlier.

THE BLIND SPOT, as its own section. All three sub-checks key on
CHANGE — blame dates, tracker status, marker words. None reads the
vocabulary registries, so none can find a docstring naming a retired
edge type or symbol kind: that docstring may never have aged relative
to its body. This was the dominant defect family in the 2026-08-18
run, one instance had escaped into the published docs/schema.json, and
correcting the flagged files surfaced 21 more sites in nine files the
scan cannot flag. Documented with the manual grep to run until
WI-sipuk lands the automated check, and with the instruction to verify
each hit against the construction site and registry first — in that
run the code was correct everywhere and only prose was stale, but that
has to be confirmed, not assumed.

TWO MORE MEASUREMENT ARTIFACTS (now four):
- Calendar decay in the day-delta arm, with the 28 -> 87 measurement
  and the DO-NOT-REBUILD note on the refuted sweep-skipper.
- Function-docstring edits re-flag the file: the scan's "body" region
  includes every non-module docstring, so fixing one moves body_age
  while ds stays put. This put linkers/ipc.py in the post-audit six
  despite an accurate module docstring. A newly-flagged file is not
  evidence of drift.

TRACKER-REF FALSE POSITIVES, both benign, so reviewers budget for them
rather than counting them as findings: extensibility notes beside a
closed ID, and mechanism words colliding with status words ("is
deferred instead" naming defer_bare_method_call). Explicitly says not
to reword correct prose to dodge the heuristic.

OPERATIONAL HAZARDS:
- Never split a module docstring's FIRST line — generate-architecture
  publishes it verbatim as the ARCHITECTURE.md summary, so wrapping it
  truncates the published doc mid-clause. That happened in the
  2026-08-18 run. Ships with a guard; the guard was executed against a
  positive control (fires on a split first line, silent on a one-liner)
  rather than pasted untested.
- Ruff RUF002 rejects ambiguous Unicode (a multiplication sign) in new
  docstring prose.
- Do not truncate the verifying grep. `grep | head` is exactly how that
  run reported a sweep as complete with 21 sites remaining.

Plus real fan-out costs (concurrency is capped and excess launches are
REJECTED, not queued; 45 agents over four waves took ~35 min) and the
two prompt details that changed answer quality: read IN FULL, and quote
contradicting code with line numbers.

Standing caveat added to the calibration section: the flagged count
measures the SCAN, not the tree. A low count is evidence about
co-change drift only.

